          "type": "turn",
          "question": "From what age does guidance describe screening for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nAccording to the national diabetes association standards of care and preventive services guidance (document: \"Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds\"), all adults are screened from age 35.\n\nYounger adults are screened too, but only if they are overweight or obese and have at least one extra risk factor. Examples in the guidance include a first-degree relative with diabetes, high blood pressure, polycystic ovary syndrome, physical inactivity, or a history of gestational diabetes. For people of Asian ancestry, the guidance uses a lower body-weight threshold.\n\nIf the screening result is normal, the usual repeat interval is three years. It may be shorter if risk factors build up or weight goes up.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not include the diagnostic thresholds themselves. The section that lists them was cut off, so I can't tell you the test values here. A clinician can explain what the screening results mean for you.",
